{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 27, :comments ["<b>In the image of Elohim.</b> The word Elohim, by way of likeness, is said of every intellectual substance in actual perfection, separate from matter, and in this respect it is necessarily eternal. Therefore it is said of the blessed El and of His angels, and likewise it is said of judges, on account of the intellectual faculty fittingly present in them. Now, the human intellect — even though the activity of its intellection employs no material instrument, and extends over the non-sensible and over certain future things, and does not weaken when its intellection increases nor with the body's old age but rather gains strength, and from all this it is clear that it is separate from matter without doubt, for indeed the opposite of all these befalls the body's material powers — nevertheless, before it contemplates, being then deprived of every perfection for which it was prepared, it is not called Elohim but only the image of Elohim, until it acquires perfection, particularly in the wisdom through which it acquires love and fear of El. For then it becomes an intellectual substance in actual perfection, separate from matter, and from this it follows that it must be eternal and enduring even after the death of the body. And since it lies within man's choice to acquire this perfection by striving to contemplate in the aforementioned wisdom, it is clear that when he withholds himself from this, his intellectual faculty — though alive — remains deprived of all actual perfection, just as it was at the beginning, and it passes into void and perishes, as it says: אָדָם בִּיקָר וְלֹא יָבִין נִמְשַׁל כַּבְּהֵמוֹת נִדְמוּ (Psalms 49:21). And all this the blessed El indicated in two words by saying: בְּצֶלֶם אֱלֹהִים."], :book :genesis, :voice :sforno, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T16:12:29.450009777Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}